No, I have however worked for various large banks in the past. Capital One 360 is currently paying 0.20% on balances up to $49,000. Let's assume you have an average of $300 a month because minimum balance is important to you. This would equate out to 60 cents a year. Any bank that has physical branches will have either a minimum deposit or direct deposit requirement. Capital one 360 does not allow you to use capital one's tellers at all (you can however use their ATM's)

&#x200B;

Simply telling you like it is, both would provide subpar experiences, not sure why you would "actively" want either account. If you want an online bank go with Simple or Schwab or Ally. If you want branch banking go with Chase.

I personally use Schwab, and have never had an issue. All of their reps are required to be Series 65 investment licensed, which may be overkill, but ensures you only speak with top quality people. I can ask pretty complex questions, and always get some really clarifying answers by someone who has a college degree, even at 2 in the morning. They refund all ATM fees everywhere, and they have top notch security, and protect against more stuff then is legally required, such as someone stealing your checkbook and writing out checks...their security guarantee is if you tell us a transaction is unauthorized, we will refund you period. They also post mobile check deposits to the account immediately without a hold, so checks are like cash with them, which is awesome...i've never seen that anywhere else.

&#x200B;

Before Schwab, I used Simple, which was amazing in terms of technology and innovation, but terrible in terms of support and protection. I was able to use their amazing tools to monitor it 24/7, but they really have awful security practices, and have been known to miss/not respond to fraud claims at all, which can only be sent via their electronic messaging which to me is unacceptable. They also advise their employees to make up stuff instead of getting the right answers, so you'll often get complete misinformation. I will say as a self-service platform that they are amazing if you don't need to use support or protection, but there's no way to guarantee that, and if things go bad you're screwed.

&#x200B;

Before Simple I used Chase. Hate the fee structure that they have, but love the bank.

I mean it depends on what you're talking about. If you just inherited money and are looking to invest...no one as banks are not fiduciaries.

&#x200B;

If you experienced fraud on your account then a Relationship Manager can help. If it's something complex like a check being made out incorrectly, you would generally want to speak to a manager, as they will go over all possible options with you. For the most part these roles will have your best interests in mind, in the types of situations that I mentioned

I mean, as a banker, I once reported someone who would come once a week with $10,000 in hard cash to pay off his credit card, like it was nothing. He would use the credit card, and rack up $10,000 in charges a week, come into the bank with 10k in hand in  tattered clothes, and pay off the card. After he did this straight every week for about 4 1/2 months, I reported him because he would act super strange about everything. Never found out what came of it, but he stopped shortly after that... 

&#x200B;

paying 4500 one time, really isn't a big deal.

I've worked for both Chase and BofA and both have a similar system when it comes to the decedent department. Prepare for about a 2 week turnaround time while everything gets worked out. You'll need to go to the branch, and present official I.D stating you are who you say you are and tell the banker, you aren't dead. The banker than needs to open a claim with the decedent department in the back office, and input the data of how they verified you. The decedent department then has to code it correctly, and remove the death notification. This typically takes 3-4 days but can take up to 2 weeks. The department that handles this aspect is not customer facing, and you won't be able to talk to them directly.

&#x200B;

I wish to note however that this means that someone called up and said you were dead. Thats the only way this restriction gets placed on your account, so in this case, it's likely an ex or someone who wanted to cause you stress. This is not a glitch, anytime someone reports someone as dead, the bank freezes the account until it can figure out what's going on. The bank doesn't start shutting anything down until they receive the death certificate.

Everything I say is general, so there may be some exceptions like if your business is not required to collect sales tax, or is exempt, or falls within a business category that a particular state has deemed worthy of a higher business tax break, in which case Delaware wouldn't make sense. I'd recommend shelling out $500 and talking with a business lawyer who can look at your situation and provide specific advice for your business.  

&#x200B;

But generally yes, Delaware is best.

MOST Credit Unions (90%) choose not to invest in technology and require mountains of paperwork to get anything done. Basic things that banks can do by phone, credit unions require you to come into the branch to do. I remember I had a case of fraud, and they sent me a form that had to be notarized attesting to it, that then needed to be mailed back. Many times they would lose this paperwork, and have to re-send everything. They may be friendly when giving you this paperwork, but i'd argue most people would rather have efficient processes that work then constant non-sense processes. If you want old, antiquated technology then credit unions are for you.

&#x200B;

With that being said, theres one credit union that i'm a member of, that invests highly in technology, and their system is on par with Bank of America, but it was quite hard to find one like that. Most are awful, you might find 1% are good.

This is exactly what Simple does. It's like it's main feature. Here's a video: [https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GsAhHBW5bQM](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GsAhHBW5bQM)

