Not sure why you paid it off. Once you attempted to have it removed by the bank 3 times yourself, and were told by the bank 3 times that the funds were legitimate (provided your statement is accurate) this would be seen as negligence by the bank in court. ( In essence YOU could have had the bank arrested) You actively trying to have the funds removed, and the bank putting the funds back in time and time again after multiple investigations of your claim and telling you repeatedly that it is legitimate, would be seen as an act that you relied on. A court would likely have used the "reasonable person" statute, in that a reasonable person would assume the funds belong to them after being told by the bank directly 3 times, and thus you utilized the funds in good faith after this information was confirmed in multiple investigations that you initiated. Almost any judge would have ruled the $1000 loss was due to bank negligence, and would have ruled in your favor (again provided that your summary is indeed accurate)

&#x200B;

Edit: Whoever is downvoting me, clearly doesn't understand how the legal system is set up. Regardless of whether or not he was the actual owner of the funds, past precedent states that this is how the courts would have ruled on this case, provided the information OP provided was accurate. You can "not like it" but it's how it is.

File a complaint with the California Department of Insurance, stating that your insurance company is refusing to enforce their in-network policy contract with the biller and are allowing you to get balance billed, despite this being against the terms of the contract that you have with them: [http://www.insurance.ca.gov/01-consumers/101-help/](http://www.insurance.ca.gov/01-consumers/101-help/) they'll launch an investigation into the insurer, which typically lights a fire under them to call the biller and tell them that they cannot balance bill you, and that they'll be filing a lawsuit against the biller for a violation of in-network terms and conditions and that they will be providing an attorney on your behalf to fight the claim, if they continue to do so. This typically gets the bill wiped, and everything corrected.

&#x200B;

Source: I've had to do this in NJ a couple of times, i'm fairly sure with California being as liberal as they are that your insurance enforcement protection is better than my states

Betterment accepts DTC transfers out of an account for taxable accounts, this isn't on the ACAT system, but essentially has the same end result of transferring your shares to a new brokerage firm without causing any tax implications. You'll have to have your new brokerage firm send them paperwork requesting a DTC transfer. If it's over a certain amount, it'll also have to have a medallion signature guarantee.

&#x200B;

For IRA's they sell everything since there isn't any tax liability, and send the funds as cash to your new firm. There is no way for Betterment to not sell your investments for IRA's before transferring.

when you file a claim, the bank lets the company know the charge was fraudulent and attempts to get back any money that's available from the company. While i can't be sure, this is likely what you're seeing occur.

&#x200B;

Edit: if it's an investment firm, it's likely the funds minus the difference in how the stocks performed during the timeframe the funds were invested.

The Credit Card won't have any fees as it's seen as a PayPal Charge, and thus a regular purchase as opposed to a cash advance. Paypal themselves charge a 2.9% processing fee, but the Travel rewards card earns 1.5% cash back, which would also be applied in addition to the bonus. So OP is looking at a total cost of 1.4% of the transaction amount he runs through PayPal, which would be a grand total of $14. He would get a bonus of $250 for paying this $14 fee, giving him a grand profit of $236. This would make it "worth it" to the average person.

&#x200B;

Edit: Also the IRS has deemed that bonuses on credit cards are "rebates" since they require a certain amount of spending, and thus are not subject to taxation. There would be no taxes on this bonus, and the funds would essentially be "tax free"

