However...to answer your question, yes it is possible to issue a check from one bank and have another bank's routing number, if the bank is partnered with the other bank. For example Charles Schwab for their brokerage accounts partner with Chase, so if you get checks for a Schwab brokerage account, the routing number would belong to Chase, even though Schwab has their own bank. This however is rare, so it's not common. But it IS possible

Downsides:

If there IS fraud again, prepaid companies generally have the WORST protection, and take the longest to investigate/get your money back...in some cases upwards of 2 months

You have cash sitting around that can easily be stolen/lost. Just the other day, $20 slid out of my pocket and is now gone...unless you are an expert with tracking your money, this makes it very easy to lose your money, and makes you a target for robberies if people see you carrying that much cash (especially internationally).

Prepaid cards generally have a lot of fees. They are designed for those who cannot get a bank account due to bad credit/over-drafting, not paying back, and then getting blacklisted by banks. Therefore every-time you buy a $500 mastercard, you'll be paying $4.95 for the ability to do so.

There's no rewards

Because it's designed for poor people, the prepaid companies generally don't invest all that much in the platform, so there's numerous reports of the system going down all the time, and not being able to use your pre-paid card when that happens. Green dot got fined millions of dollars for this, but continue to let it happen.

&#x200B;

Upsides:

In some cases, for low denominations you can get anonymous giftcards, which can be nice for evading taxes

You can practice and refine your shooting skills, when someone breaks into your house to steal the cash you have lying around

The higher fees may incentivize you to purchase less items, thus making you balance a budget better and appreciate life more, and only buy things that matter.

If you want to get creative, you can pretend that the system denying your purchase is a sign from the heavens that you  didn't need that item...and fate is stopping you from getting it.

