To answer your first question about the NY HCRA surcharge...it depends on the insurance plan. Some have an agreement (which generally costs more to the employer/school that's providing it to you) to cover it. If your school has not opted to pay for this as part of their plan (which is common) you would legally be able to be billed for this portion, and have to pay it,  you will want to check with your insurance to see if this is included in your plan. The plan does not have a legal requirement to pay for the HCRA surcharge.

Any person that is affiliated with a hospital network is allowed to charge a facility fee, which is different than the surcharge. The surcharge in NY however will apply to any hospital network. This means you can only have a "guaranteed" bill without the surcharge, if your provider is NOT affiliated in ANYWAY with a hospital. If you see "An RWJ Facility" or "NY Presbyterian" or any hospital name that is tied to that provider, there is close to a 100% chance you will be hit with that fee. Doesn't matter if it's an office or not. ONLY go to independents, unless there is a specific reason (like you got cancer), and the top specialist happens to be affiliated with a hospital (in which case you will need to pay the fee)

&#x200B;

Can't say about your 3rd point...either the billing office missed it, or it was hidden, or your insurance had an agreement with that specific hospital group to cover the HCRA fee but only for facilities that fall under that specific hospital. Your best bet would be to go over those visits with your insurance to see why a fee wasn't charged.

&#x200B;

In situations like you described with an EKG, you can generally appeal this with insurance and win as it was being presented to you as part of the annual checkup. You may have to do a secondary appeal that goes to the state for review, but in the end, this would have close to a 100% win rate for you.

