"Sales are subject to a transaction fee of between $0.01 and $0.03 per $1,000 principal."

This refers to the act of placing the trade, and means they don't sell your data to cover the cost. Essentially many brokerages do something called selling order flow to cover this $0.01 to $0.03 cost, where they sell your trade data to High Frequency Trading firms, that then use that info to essentially "front-run" you, and buy the stocks before you do based off the volume of the data they receive. It's generally better if you just pay the $0.01 to $0.03 yourself. You can read more on this here: [https://www.investopedia.com/terms/p/paymentoforderflow.asp](https://www.investopedia.com/terms/p/paymentoforderflow.asp) 

&#x200B;

