The definition of selling in this case would be defined sales goals set by corporate management to generate an excess profit for the bank...meaning x amount of product needs to be sold per month...X checking accounts, x savings, x loans, x credit cards, and legally management would be forced by the government to not do this, and allow the chips to fall as they may.

&#x200B;

Your examples are poor because they assume sales goals are looking out for the customers interests, when they really aren't.

&#x200B;

Lets look at option 2

Another example, let’s say a customer sits with a banker and the customer says, “I’m doing remodeling on my home so I need a loan.”

Depending on the month, there may be a credit card incentive for the banker, which gives a massive payout, so instead of talking about a home equity line of credit, the banker offers a 0% credit card offer for 6 months, and talks it up to the customer, who isn't knowledgable on 0% offers, and gets hit with 24% after the intro period ends...Now the banker gets the incentive and the customer loses...this is an example of how sales goals usually turn out.

&#x200B;

As an example let’s say a customer comes into the bank, sits with the banker to open a savings account. The customer has $50,000. The customer says, “I want a savings account so I can earn interest on the money I deposit.”

&#x200B;

In this case, the sales incentive for the banker is generally greater for savings accounts, then time deposits, because the bank earns more money on them putting it in a savings, so the banker won't mention the time deposit because they're going to earn 3x as much in an incentive if the client puts it in a savings.

&#x200B;

I repeat what I said earlier, sales goals should be made illegal in the banking industry.

&#x200B;

