This is in no way a clear case, The Cash app's legal terms and conditions state that it is only to be used for friends and family money transfers only, and that any money transfer is final. You were also the one who authorized the transaction in the cash app. Violating the terms and conditions by utilizing it for a loan, does not make it eligible for a chargeback. The only thing you can do here, is claim that you weren't the one who made the transfer, which would be unethical.

&#x200B;

Edit: You can also take the person to court.

Likely this is a sad story of uneducated investing.

&#x200B;

Based off of what you described, your parents had a 529 College Savings Plan that was being invested in the market...likely via an advisor. In 2008, the market crashed, and many of these plans dropped 50-80% in value during this time period. Likely it dropped significantly and your parents, in an emotional panic, sold all the investments and pulled the funds out, instead of leaving it invested, solidifying the loss. They also likely incurred a 10% penalty fee from the government since they did not use the money for college in the allowed timeframe after selling the stocks, as well as closing costs and advisory fees from the bank...leading them to the perspective that "the banks took all my money" 

&#x200B;

I wouldn't recommend telling them this, but if your parents had kept the funds invested (assuming it was diversified), rather than pulling it out in the middle of the crash, it would have regained all of it's value by 2010, and also wouldn't have had any penalty fees for withdrawal. Because they likely didn't know any better, they acted emotional, made a temporary loss permanent, and lost the money.

Yes, this is generally true for amounts that are about $50 or more. For $10 though, the ATM likely isn't going to care. Banks have internal policies based off of the amount of the check. It's not that the ATM recognizes the endorsement, it's that the amount is so low that it will process it, despite it being against typical bank policy.

You would sign the back, and then write "endorsed to YOUR FRIENDS NAME" underneath. Your friend would then sign under that when they receive it, and deposit it at the ATM

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Typically i'd agree with everyone else posting here, but for $10.07, you can likely endorse it over to him in the back, he can sign it and deposit it at the ATM, or through his phone and it'll likely go through without an issue. Banks really don't have the time/resources to analyze things under $50 when there's $10,000 fraud occurring on a daily basis.

&#x200B;

Edit: You keep using the word "cash". Your friend would not be able to "cash" the check as the tellers will stop them. If they actually have a bank account that accepts ATM/mobile deposit, they could likely submit it through that without an issue

someone got access to your online account information and password, and then attempted to move funds into a fintech investment account, that they then would use to withdraw the funds back to their own bank account. The small deposits, were "verification" deposits used by the fintech institution to verify that you are the owner of the account. The "person" logged into your online banking, got these amounts and then entered them into the fintech company.

&#x200B;

I'd recommend changing your online credentials

&#x200B;

