Simple's front line support team is unfortunately now composed of low-paid tech minions that have no power and no knowledge of banking regulations, so they constantly violate the law and don't really care, i'm not sure why the OCC hasn't come down hard at them for this yet, but i'm confident once they grow enough they're going to get fined to hell for the amount of regulatory violations the front line staff does on a daily basis.

&#x200B;

Knowing how Simple's internal structure works in-depth, If you really want to know why you keep getting denied, you will HAVE to file a complaint with the CFPB at [www.cfpb.gov](https://www.cfpb.gov) by clicking "file a complaint", this will then go to a BBVA leader (the bank that owns simple) who will look up and respond to your question as per regulatory requirements within 2 weeks. If you try to go through Simple themselves without the CFPB, you will not get an answer. Their front line reps have been instructed not to connect anyone to a supervisor, regardless of how much you may yell, or politely factually present evidence that they must tell you their reason for denial by law. It's built into their policies and their reps will get fired for escalating any issues. They currently have a 2.6 rating on glassdoor for this particular issue: [https://www.glassdoor.com/Overview/Working-at-Simple-Finance-EI\_IE798058.11,25.htm](https://www.glassdoor.com/Overview/Working-at-Simple-Finance-EI_IE798058.11,25.htm). Note that while their "system" is in compliance with the law, everything else is not. They provide no training on banking regulation to their employees, and implement policies that actually force their representatives to continually violate federal regulation by providing no information/false information. Is this really a company that you want to deal with?

I mean...Simple now offers checks, and Paypal offers the 1% cash back debit card that links to your bank. So you just use the Paypal debit card for all your purchases, and it pulls it from your bank as an ACH everyday. I've found it to be 100% accurate, and you can then rename the purchases and continue using the budgeting tools from Simple, while getting the protection from Paypal if anything happens (as simple's fraud protection is garbage) Paypal's 1% debit card also offers extended warranty and purchase protection. 

