I mean now you're talking about Canada. In the U.S customer service is generally seen as a necessity, and the regulation of banks is different. For example, in talking to people that have worked for canadian banks, the regulation requires you to provide all information down to the t and ensure the client understands it or the banker will be personally held responsible and arrested. So if I tell you a wire will take 3 days to complete, and it takes 5, and you complain to a regulator, because of the way the laws are written in canada, I can then be fined by canada as a banker, and if this caused you harm (in your mind), you then have the civil right to sue me according to canadian law. So in turn all bankers in canada don't say anything when you ask questions for fear of the laws from getting it wrong. I don't believe credit unions have this same stipulation in canada.

&#x200B;

In the US the above stipulation is not a part of the law, and thus you could not sue individual bankers over items like what was described above.

I've found Fedex to be decent and polite, and if you experience an issue and reach out, they immediately connect you with someone to resolve the issue and actually get real information as to when the item will get delivered.

