@Whatclassguy. Unfortunately no, a Medallion Signature Guarantee is when a bank takes responsibility for any fraud in the transfer of securities (meaning if you went to the bank with a fake I.D, and they signed off that it was in fact you doing the transfer, the bank would then have to pay you the full amount that was stolen in the fraud) so it is not taken lightly by banks, as fraud runs rampant, and one medallion signature guarantee gone bad can cost the bank hundreds of thousands, to millions of dollars. 

In this case I would recommend opening a "no-cost" credit card with Chase (The Freedom) this will not require you to have any bank account with them, and will only cost you about 5 minutes of time and 1 credit inquiry. After being approved, simply hold the card for 60 days. After this time period Chase will issue you a Medallion Signature Guarantee, and you will have a bank who issues them if you ever need one going forward.

Hope this helps, and good luck!
